  "Number": 40,
  "Question": "The limit of \\(\\dfrac{x^2-1}{x-1}\\) as \\(x\\) approaches \\(1\\) as a limit is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0\\)",
    "B": "Indeterminate",
    "C": "\\(x-1\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(1\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "A limit concerns values near \\(x=1\\), so the expression need not be defined at \\(1\\) itself. For \\(x\\ne1\\), factor the difference of squares and cancel the nonzero factor \\(x-1\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{x^2-1}{x-1}\n=\\frac{(x-1)(x+1)}{x-1}=x+1.\n\\]\nAs \\(x\\) approaches \\(1\\) from either side, \\(x+1\\) approaches \\(2\\). Indeed, at every allowed input its distance from \\(2\\) is exactly \\(|x-1|\\). Thus the limit is \\(\\boxed{2}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{D}}\\).",
